  "title": "Two broadcasts tie at one instant in the fallback: the one whose first copy has the smaller delivery key is taken",
  "rules": ["lnk_02"],
  "reasoning": [
    "lnk_02's fallback takes the position nearest in time within 30 minutes, \"the earlier on a tie\". Casey, PR #72 (his answer to PR #79, Q1), verbatim: \"(a), the broadcast whose first copy has the smaller delivery key.\" A broadcast's copies count as one position timed by its first copy (Casey, PR #72, Q3).",
    "s14-r sent two broadcasts. Packet 14005, its cell c20 about 1 km north, reached gateway s14-h1 at 23 10:20:00 (s14-m005, the first copy) and gateway s14-h2 at 10:20:02 (s14-m002). Packet 14004, its cell r20, reached s14-r's own uplink at 10:20:00 (s14-m004). Both broadcasts are timed 10:20:00.",
    "The receipt at 10:30 (s14-m006, a direct hop at s14-g) reads both broadcasts in its 7-day look-back; c20 and r20 neither overlap nor lie within 40 m (their stored values are 1000.315 m apart): moved. The fallback finds both 10 minutes before the receipt, at the same instant, so the tie goes to the smaller delivery key of each broadcast's first copy: s14-m004 (r20) before s14-m005 (c20). Taking the smallest key over all copies instead would compare s14-m002 with s14-m004 and take c20.",
    "The receipt uses r20 at 2745.603337 m from g16 and is counted. Its hour has 1 packet: dropped by lnk_04."
  ],
